course: dictionary
lesson: 5466
title: Laptop, a computer you carry
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A laptop is a small computer that you can carry about. Its screen and keyboard are joined together, and they fold shut like a book. When you meet the word laptop, picture a computer you could carry from room to room.
explain: The word is put together from two parts you already know: lap and top. Your lap is the flat part across your legs when you sit down, and a laptop is often used there. Check it with context: «Zara plays on an old, slow laptop» tells you it is a computer she plays games on, so it must be a computer.
explain: Read the other sentences for more clues. «Press a key on the laptop and a letter pops up on the screen» shows the keyboard and screen working together. «A fan blows air over metal fins, which is why a laptop roars during a big game» shows that a laptop has hot parts inside that need cooling.
fact: A laptop is a small computer that you can carry.
fact: A laptop has a screen and a keyboard joined together, and they fold shut.
fact: Pupils at school use laptops for research, typing and coding.
fact: Keep passwords secret, use only the websites your teacher allows, and tell an adult if something online upsets you.
fact: A laptop runs on a battery that needs charging; keep drinks away from it and carry it closed.
exercise: «Zara plays on an old, slow laptop.» What is a laptop? | A soft cushion you sit on | *A small computer you can carry, with a screen and keyboard that fold shut | A large computer that stays in one room | A phone with no screen
exercise: In school, what do pupils use laptops for? | Cooking dinner | Playing football outside | Sleeping under the desk | *Research, typing and coding
exercise: Which part of a laptop do you type on? | *The keyboard | The metal fins | The fan | The battery
test: Why should you keep drinks away from a laptop? | Drinks make it run faster | It makes the screen bigger | Laptops need juice to work | *Spilt drink could damage it
test: What does «charge the laptop» mean? | Lift it up high | Paint the screen | Count its keys | *Put power into its battery
exam: Which is a good online safety rule for a laptop? | Visit any website you like | Hide problems from your teacher | Share your password with your friends | *Keep your password secret
